        revenueText: "Industrial electronics, which is mostly Kavach, was **₹1,626cr, about 49% of FY26 revenue, against ₹298cr (15%) in FY25**; batteries were ₹1,416cr (43%, flat on the year) and defence and aviation ₹212cr (6%). Kavach alone was almost half of FY26 sales; management expects a similar share in FY27, a good FY28 and a decline from FY29. Exports were about ₹528cr, mostly batteries.",
        mixes: [
            { caption: 'Revenue by segment, FY26 (annual report)', parts: [{ label: 'Industrial electronics (mostly Kavach)', share: 49 }, { label: 'Batteries', share: 43 }, { label: 'Defence and aviation', share: 6 }, { label: 'Other', share: 2 }], note: 'Shares of ₹3,311cr of consolidated segment revenue.' },
        ],
        customers: ["**Indian Railways**, through Kavach, signalling and battery orders. Kavach trackside work has started on 21,794 route kilometres, but only 2,633 were commissioned by July 2026, and FY27's budget for it is ₹2,066cr.", "**Defence and aviation buyers** for military batteries and fuzes, and **telecom and power companies** for industrial batteries."],
        difficulty: { level: 'Very high', text: "Kavach must meet the railways' highest safety-integrity level, pass an independent safety assessment and survive years of RDSO trials. HBL has worked on it with the railways' design body since 2005 and was among the first two of about five vendors cleared for version 4.0." },
        moat: "Kavach is effectively a government-created oligopoly in a safety-critical product, where the buyer cannot afford to experiment with a new supplier. The weakness is dependence on one programme and one buyer, which peaks within a few years.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Kavach rollout pace**: the order book was ₹2,999cr at 31 December 2025 (CARE; ₹4,479cr in July 2025) and a ₹1,714cr Kavach locomotive order followed on 28 May 2026; management expects good years in FY27 and FY28 and a decline from FY29 as competition bites.", "**Big-order swings**: the September 2025 quarter earned ₹387cr as a large locomotive order peaked; that order closed at 76% delivered, and quarterly profit fell to ₹64cr in March 2026 and ₹109cr in June.", "**Railway and telecom spending** on standby power, which drives the older battery business.", "**New electric drive trains** for heavy trucks and boats, which are still pre-revenue and a year or more from pilot sales."],
        inputs: "**Lead, nickel and cadmium for batteries, and electronic components and chips for Kavach.** Materials were 42% of sales in FY26, down from 61% in FY22 as higher-value electronics took over the mix.",
        costEffect: "**Mostly insulated today, because electronics dominate.** Kavach earns far more on engineering than on materials; the battery business is more exposed to metal prices. The company does not explain its pass-through terms in our sources. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 4 points (34% to 30%). **What happened:** India's lead price index rose about 9% from Q1 FY24 to now, yet the quarterly operating margin went from about 17% to 23%. The driver was Kavach, not metal: the margin hit 44.5% in the September 2025 quarter and fell to 12% in the March 2026 quarter as Kavach deliveries came and went.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'Medha Servo Drives, Kernex Microsystems', note: 'Other approved Kavach suppliers (about five were cleared for version 4.0); orders are won by competitive bid.' }, { name: 'Exide Industries, Amara Raja', note: 'Much larger battery makers competing in industrial batteries.' }],
        position: "**Among the first two of about five vendors cleared for Kavach 4.0**, with an order book of ₹2,999cr at December 2025 (CARE) before a ₹1,714cr locomotive order in May 2026, and a long-standing niche supplier of specialised batteries to railways and defence.",
        money: "Net cash of **₹887cr** (Screener, March 2026). Capital spending was about ₹144cr in FY26, funded from its own cash; no equity has been raised. Bank guarantees of ₹303cr are outstanding, it holds a ₹92.75cr Kavach warranty provision, and the auditor flagged the estimates behind its percentage-of-completion revenue. Shareholders were asked to approve up to ₹700cr a year of dealings with subsidiaries, a Cochin Shipyard joint venture and associates. Receivables rose by about ₹285cr in FY26 as Kavach billing grew. On 9 October 2026 CARE upgraded its long-term rating to AA- (Stable) from A+, citing growth and Kavach execution. Promoters hold **59%**, unchanged over three years.",
