        difficulty: { level: 'Medium', text: "Assembling cameras is not the hard part: the chips come from Taiwan and assembly lines can be bought. What is hard is passing the government's STQC cyber-security testing for every model, securing chips in a worldwide shortage, and building a brand and installer network across India. Kadapa can make about 2.5 million units a month and runs at 85-90% of that." },
        moat: "The brand is almost a byword for CCTV in India, backed by the widest range of certified products, its own factory and nationwide reach. The weakness is that part of the lead came from a rule: more than 40 certified brands still compete, and a policy change on Chinese suppliers would reopen the market.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Security spending by businesses, homes and government**: cameras are becoming standard in shops, offices, housing societies and public projects. Management guides FY27 revenue of ₹6,000-6,500cr, against ₹4,221cr in FY26.", "**The STQC testing rule**: from April 2025 CCTV products could no longer use Chinese chips or memory, and the last old stock had to be sold by 1 April 2026. Its market share roughly doubled to 43.3% (Frost & Sullivan).", "**Price increases**: memory and chip costs have jumped worldwide, so prices are up 10-20% so far and heading to about 25% for FY27. Part of this year's revenue growth is price, not volume.", "**Shift to IP cameras and premium products**: higher-priced network cameras are 79% of the range, and the Pro series goes after contracts held by global brands.", "**Capacity**: the Kadapa plant is nearly full, so capacity is being doubled over three years at Kadapa and a second cluster in Greater Noida."],
        inputs: "**Chips and electronic parts**: processors and memory from Taiwan (about 35% of the bill of materials) and passive components from Taiwan and China (15-20%), plus lenses, plastic and metal housings, connectors and cables, which it is starting to make in India. Materials were 71% of sales in FY26.",
        costEffect: "**Partly passed on, with a lag.** It raises prices monthly or quarterly rather than all at once, lines up 3-4 quarters of chip supply in advance, and hedges 85-100% of its foreign-currency exposure every week. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 7 points (13% to 6%). **What happened:** India's circuit-board price index barely moved (100 to 102 from Q1 FY25), while the quarterly margin rose from 6.4% to 14.6%. Materials fell from 82% to 71% of sales over two years as its own brand and IP cameras replaced low-margin trading and it took share after the testing rule; Q4 FY26 (18.1%) was also helped by cheap old stock. The memory-chip price surge does not show in that index but is the real cost pressure now.",
